  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nWe continue to have this strong ridge across the southeast into the \nend of the work week with high heat (18C+ 850 mb temps that are \nconducive to 90F sfc temps with mixing and 70 degree dew points) \nthat look to continue until we can get some weather in here or a \nweakness forms in the upper ridging. \n\nWe do have the risk of severe weather today (Slight risk from SPC) \nthat with the high heat and instability could produce some severe \nweather, primarily west of IN-15 and north of US-6 where the better \nshear seems to reside. The better flow is north and west of the area \nand the NAM seems to want to spark some stronger convection in the \n18z window across WI today and move it southeast into the evening. \nIf we can get some good convergence to get some ascent within the \nbetter jet kinematics to the north, that would seem to be enough to \nerode the cap and spark some thunderstorms across the area. An \noutflow boundary pushes into the area and pushes southeastward and \nlikely becomes a focus for storms this afternoon. The HRRR and the \nRAP don't seem too enthusiastic about it though and so will continue \nwith the low 20 to 30 percent chance PoPs that have a threat for \nwind, hail. \n\nThis puts the area in a bit of a bind for what to do for Friday as \nfar as heat headlines go. Since this depends on if overnight \nconvection can happen or not, do not have a lot of confidence. \nIndividual model guidance seems to place the best chance (35 to 40%) \nfor heat headlines across Pulaski and Fulton, White to Miami, and \nperhaps Starke county as well, in Indiana for Friday, but there is \nsome chance for areas along and south of US-30. This would be mostly \nremoved from where thunderstorms are possible on Friday at least \nwhere model QPF is being resolved. The issue is that models are all \nover the place with the location of the low level jet for Friday, \nwhich also provides lower confidence for heat headlines. After \ncollaboration with neighboring counties have settled with the first \nfive counties mentioned above in a heat advisory for Friday where \nthe highest confidence resides with respect to storm potential and \nheat index forecast. It is possible that some tweaking takes \nplace in following forecast shifts once storm potential and \ncloud debris becomes better realized. \n\nThe NAM and GFS have model confluence around a low level jet (of \ndifferent magnitudes) diving southeast through the area, likely in \nconjunction with a convective wave Friday evening/night. The ECMWF \ndoes resolve some QPF associated with the low level warm advection, \nbut those are lower in magnitude than the GFS and NAM providing \nlower confidence again. \n\nThere does appear to be better model agreement around a backdoor \ncold front that would bring heat relief and push thunderstorm \nchances farther southwest as the upper ridging buckles and a mid \nlevel trough works into the northeast US Saturday night/Sunday time \nframe. This would keep the area dry from Sunday through Tuesday.\n\nWe're back into the high heat and humidity by Wednesday as another \nridge builds into the Western CONUS and pushes eastward toward our \narea. A wave approaching for Tuesday evening/night may bring our \nnext chance for showers and storms. Another trough winds through \nThursday providing another rain and taking away the heat. It is \npossible that the heat sort of jockeys around lasts longer than just \na day, but this one day heat chance looks most likely at this\npoint.",
